WEBVTT

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.520
Imperial cruisers and 
forty military uniforms?

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:12.170
Yes. We do have some Imperial 
ships we captured before, right?

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:13.540
As for the uniforms...

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.920
Just try to find them.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.960
Don't make it sound so easy.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.390
I realize it's to prevent 
intelligence leaks,

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.710
but still, making demands without even 
telling me the plan you need them for?

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.300
Now, now. I'll treat you 
to a glass of cognac.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.540
Not good enough. Make it three.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.300
Consider it done.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:39.220
Oh, there's one other 
thing I need to ask for.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.140
What is it?

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:43.010
Given what's happened with 
the Patriotic Knight Corps,

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.630
I'm concerned about leaving 
Julian alone while I'm away.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.610
Would it be possible to dispatch 
the MP to the area?

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.570
Well, of course. That's a simple matter.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.750
But is leaving him in someone 
else's care out of the question?

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.270
I did consider that option, 
of course, but...

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.480
I refuse.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.750
Without me, this house will fall to ruin.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.480
It'll get so dusty inside, 
it'll be unlivable.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:13.090
While you're away, I will 
assume the responsibility

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.230
of protecting this house as the 
commander in your absence.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.070
So... I just couldn't do it.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.840
That's one dependable kid!

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.140
Got it. I'll arrange them right away.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.570
I appreciate it.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:27.330
Sure.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.310
Now... let's get to the topic at hand.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.220
The 13th Fleet Personnel 
Distribution Plan...

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:06.040
<b>Legend of the Galactic Heroes</b>

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:12.200
<i>Shortly thereafter, the officers 
chosen by Yang and Cazerne</i>

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.740
<i>came together under the 
banner of the 13th Fleet.</i>

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.830
<i>For vice commander, they chose the 
seasoned veteran Commodore Fischer,</i>

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.050
Commodore Edwin Fischer

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.060
<i>who fought bravely in 
the Battle of Astarte.</i>

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:28.450
<i>For chief officer, they chose the 
meticulous and orderly Commodore Murai.</i>

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.100
Commodore Murai

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:35.590
<i>And for deputy chief officer, they chose 
Captain Patrichev, known as the "Fighter."</i>

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:35.060
Captain Fyodor Patrichev

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.740
I humbly accept the position.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.890
But why me?

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:45.870
Your skillful fleet management was a 
great help in the Battle of Astarte.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.540
Our fleet is comprised of the survivors 
of the 4th and 6th Fleets,

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.900
in addition to new recruits.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.820
This necessitates highly 
elaborate coordination.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.300
I see. That is a heavy responsibility, indeed.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.510
Good to see you again.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.230
Glad to see you're doing well.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.210
As well as ever.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.490
I haven't gotten any better at 
three-dimensional chess, either.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.200
I haven't seen you since Econia.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.140
I didn't expect you to appoint 
me to this position.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:24.110
I always wanted to call on 
you if anything came up.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:29.210
He was completely enamored by you 
after what happened on Econia.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.700
If I am to command a fleet, I need 
a man like you on my staff.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.400
I humbly request your aid.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.320
With pleasure.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.430
By the way, as for your adjutant...

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.460
Did you find anyone?

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:47.870
I did.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:52.140
Graduated second-in-class from 
cadet school, class of 794.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.700
A student far superior to you.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:58.870
He's assigned to the Integrated Tactical 
Operations Center's Information Analysis Division,

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.000
with a reputation for having an 
extraordinarily strong memory.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.400
Things are busy over there right now,

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.650
so I'll arrange an introduction later.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.870
<b>Episode 6</b>

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.870
<b>The Capture of Iserlohn (Part 1)</b>

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.860
What to do...

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:30.970
Sub-lieutenant Frederica Greenhill

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.470
I am Sub-lieutenant Frederica Greenhill.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:31.510
I have been appointed as adjutant 
to Rear Admiral Yang.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:33.650
R-Right...

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.980
Rear Admiral Cazerne ordered me to come.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.040
Well, this is unexpected.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.730
Unexpected? What is?

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:47.270
Nothing. Never mind.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.190
Your name is Greenhill? Would that mean...

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:54.410
Yes, Admiral Dwight Greenhill is my father.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.120
You certainly are far superior to me.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.880
Not at all.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.580
I am young and inexperienced, 
but I will rely on your guidance.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.360
I'm glad to have you here.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.110
Have you had a look at my strategy outline?

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:13.540
I'd like to hear your honest opinion.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.260
It will be successful.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.720
Why are you so confident?

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:25.430
You were successful at El Facil 
eight years ago, as well.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.410
That's a rather flimsy basis 
for such conviction...

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:37.720
But, at that time, you successfully planted 
absolute faith in a young girl's mind.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:43.170
I was at that airport on 
El Facil with my mother.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.620
I'm sure you've long since forgotten me,

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.550
as well as what you said after 
you were saved by drinking

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.060
the coffee I gave you.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:55.560
What did I say?

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:57.760
"You're a big help.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:02.530
I don't like coffee, though, 
so I wish you had brought tea."

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.480
I said something that rude?

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.770
Yes, you did.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.200
I see. I apologize.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:17.420
But I feel there must be far more beneficial 
uses for that keen memory of yours.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.790
Yes, sir. Thank you.

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:30.690
Adding new soldiers to the survivors 
of the 4th and 6th Fleets...

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.160
Yang Wen-li is only in his 
twenties and lacks experience.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.180
Can someone like that really 
bring down Iserlohn?

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.680
What was the chief thinking, anyway?

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:45.270
It's been a long time since that former 
soldier left the battlefield.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.890
Perhaps he's finally gone senile?

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:52.650
I certainly hope you men 
don't feel ashamed later.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.570
A-Admiral Bewcock!

00:07:56.330 --> 00:08:03.840
You may be making the mistake of laughing 
at a tree sapling because it lacks height.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:03.210
<u>5th Fleet Commander
</u>Vice Admiral Alexandre Bewcock

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.620
Looks like the admiral's on your side.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.960
I won't thank him, though.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.790
He'd just laugh at me.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:27.220
It is reassuring, though.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:31.440
Oh, right.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.890
I finally got your strategy outline.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.130
Glad to hear it.

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.490
It's a pretty reckless plan, though.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:39.110
I can't help feeling exasperated.

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:40.520
I'm sorry about that.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:41.920
So...

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:45.020
Who are you going to get to carry it out?

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:48.620
It'll take a real daredevil 
to pull that off.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:52.020
I actually do have someone in mind.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:00.730
The Rosen Ritter, "Knights of the Rose."

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:05.200
A ground combat force comprised 
of refugees from the Empire,

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.230
with tactical skills said to be among 
the best in all the Alliance.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:16.590
Its thirteenth regimental commander 
is Captain Walter von Schönkopf.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.450
His Imperial language won't 
be an issue, will it?

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.160
No, but there are unfavorable rumors.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:25.510
Rumors?

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:30.680
Of all their past captains, 
six defected back to the Empire.

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:33.580
There are concerns that he 
may become the seventh.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.110
Based on what?

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:38.580
Because the number thirteen 
is considered unlucky.

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:42.050
So these concerns are basically 
groundless, then.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:48.960
Do you wish to meet him? I can 
set up an appointment at once.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:49.900
No.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:52.720
I'll go see him myself.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.110
This is a tremendous favor I'm 
asking of him, after all.

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:35.750
All right. Looks like I win this one.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:38.410
That's what you said the other day, too.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.360
Looks like we've got a couple 
of fish out of water here.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.380
We are Admiral Yang Wen-li

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:54.830
and Sub-lieutenant Frederica Greenhill 
of the 13th Fleet.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:58.320
We have come to see Captain Schönkopf.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:02.040
You're Admiral Greenhill's 
daughter, aren't you?

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:05.920
The rumors were true. 
You really are a beauty.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:12.140
Not even a salute. They certainly are rude.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.030
Uh... Actually, we're here 
on urgent business.

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:22.090
We'd like you to introduce 
us to Captain Schönkopf.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:28.720
I'd be glad to tell Sub-lieutenant 
Greenhill where he is.

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:33.740
Rude behavior in front of an admiral 
will only be tolerated for so long.

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:47.940
That will be enough, Sub-lieutenant.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.530
How long are you going to keep up that act?

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.560
You're all taking your horseplay too far.

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:01.720
You are...

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:07.080
<u>Thirteenth Regiment Commander of the Rosen Ritter
</u>Captain Walter von Schönkopf

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:06.620
Captain Walter von Schönkopf.

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:23.810
Seeing someone as beautiful as you makes 
them act like little boys again.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.380
The same goes for me.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:27.540
Please forgive us.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:37.070
So, what in the world could have 
brought the hero of El Facil here?

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:39.290
I'd like to discuss something with you.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:41.530
I assume it's important.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:43.560
Most likely.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:56.250
<u>Rosen Ritter Regiment
</u>Commander Kasper Linz

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:56.250
<u>Rosen Ritter Regiment
</u>Lieutenant Rainer Blumhardt

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:00.870
Yes, I suppose there is no other way.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:05.130
Being in a strong fortress 
does make people careless.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:10.890
But if I become the seventh 
traitor, like the rumors say,

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:13.250
it will be all for naught.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.380
What would you do then?

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:17.490
I'd be stuck.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.940
Well, I'm sure you would.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:24.250
But would you stop at that?

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:27.110
Surely you've already thought 
up some countermeasure.

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.100
No, I couldn't think of anything.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:34.310
If you betray me, that'll be the 
end. I'll have no further moves.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.820
Does that mean you place 
your full trust in me?

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:41.310
The truth is, I'm not very confident.

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:47.490
But if I don't put my faith in 
you, this plan won't work.

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:51.680
So I will trust you. 
That's the first prerequisite.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:54.950
I see.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:58.970
May I ask one question?

00:13:58.970 --> 00:13:59.720
Sure.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:05.030
The order you've been given is 
impossible by its very nature.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:10.020
You have half a fleet, quite frankly 
a ragtag assembly of weaklings,

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.120
with which you're supposed 
to defeat Iserlohn.

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:17.520
No one would blame you for refusing 
to follow such an order.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:23.060
Did you agree to it because you 
already had this plan in mind?

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:29.040
Even if that is the case, I would 
like to know your true motive.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:32.280
Is it honor you want? Or a promotion?

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:35.270
I don't think it's for a promotion.

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:39.490
It's enough to be called 
"Your Excellency" before the age of thirty.

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:43.140
Besides, if I return from 
this mission alive,

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:45.510
I intend to retire from the service.

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:47.280
Retire from the service?

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:52.200
Yes, well... I would receive 
pension and retirement pay.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:56.670
Just enough to support myself 
and one other, at least.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.670
I wouldn't be left wanting.

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:01.880
You would retire in the 
current state of affairs?

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:05.750
That's the thing. It's because 
of the state of affairs.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.660
If our forces occupy Iserlohn,

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:12.330
it would cut off what is essentially 
the Empire's only route to invade us.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:17.100
So long as we don't attempt something 
as stupid as a reverse invasion,

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:20.230
neither force will be able 
to confront the other.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.110
Not on a large scale, at least.

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:27.030
It will depend on the Alliance 
government's diplomatic skill,

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:29.930
but if we can secure a location that 
offers a militaristic advantage,

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:35.390
perhaps we can forge a peace treaty with 
the Empire that would satisfy both sides.

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:40.690
And that would give me the 
peace of mind to retire.

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:45.300
But would that peace really be permanent?

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.740
Permanent peace has never existed 
in all of mankind's history.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:53.270
So I'm not hoping for that.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:58.320
But a few decades of peace gave 
us an era of prosperity.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:03.510
If we must leave something behind 
for the next generation,

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:06.240
I would like it to be peace.

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:10.940
And maintaining the peace handed to 
them by the previous generation

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:13.230
would then be their responsibility.

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:18.980
If each generation can remember their 
responsibility to future generations,

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:22.390
perhaps we can have long-term 
peace as a result.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:30.130
In short, my hope is for a few 
decades of peace, at most.

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:33.350
But even that would be tens 
of thousands of times better

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:36.190
than an era of war lasting 
a tenth that long.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:40.490
I take care of a fourteen-year-old 
boy back home,

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:43.660
and I don't want to see him sent off to battle.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.640
That's all.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:49.600
Forgive me, Admiral,

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:52.770
but you must be either the 
most honest man alive

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:56.450
or the greatest sophist since 
Emperor Rudolf himself.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:06.470
That strategy sounds like a real gamble.

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:07.830
I don't deny that.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:09.870
There are no words.

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:11.290
Perhaps not.

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:13.750
And if it fails?

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:17.210
We turn tail and run.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:19.210
But that would be...

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:20.980
Please, don't worry.

00:17:21.330 --> 00:17:23.480
This was an impossible 
mission to begin with.

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:28.200
If we fail, the only ones who'll be 
disgraced are myself and Chief Sithole.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:32.270
The admiral and I carefully 
examined this plan yesterday.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:35.140
I believe this is the only possible way.

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:39.610
Are you sure about this? You would 
have the most dangerous role.

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:45.920
If I fail and get taken prisoner, 
it would just mean going back home.

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:51.040
Pardon me.

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:53.460
I may have taken that joke too far.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.930
It may appear foolhardy,

00:17:56.930 --> 00:18:00.690
but I've thought this through 
as rationally as I could.

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.690
I won't ask anyone to 
trust me, by any means.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:07.300
I just want you to think

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:11.350
about ways to conquer Iserlohn 
with half a fleet.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.680
There may be a few possible methods,

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.850
but I believe we would all arrive 
at the same conclusion.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:22.040
Understood.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.700
We will begin preparations at once.

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:42.020
<u>13th Fleet Flagship</u>
Hyperion

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:08.070
<i>A nonnavigable sector of space exists between 
the territories of the Empire and the Alliance.</i>

00:19:08.510 --> 00:19:13.050
<i>Iserlohn Fortress is found on the one 
and only passable route through it.</i>

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:13.250
Iserlohn Fortress

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:18.050
<i>At this fortress are two Imperial admirals.</i>

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:22.900
<i>One is the fortress's commander, 
Admiral Stockhausen.</i>

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:23.180
<u>Iserlohn Fortress Commander
</u>Admiral Thoma von Stockhausen

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:28.440
<i>The other is the commander of the 
fortress garrison, Admiral Seeckt.</i>

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:28.430
<u>Iserlohn Fortress Garrison Commander
</u>Admiral Hans Dietrich von Seeckt

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:34.040
<i>The existence of two commanders 
with identical ranks</i>

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.250
<i>has created a heated antagonism.</i>

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.200
<i>The two men scorn and curse one another,</i>

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.650
<i>but whenever the Alliance launches an attack,</i>

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:44.710
<i>their refusal to let the other have the glory</i>

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:49.480
<i>results in tremendous gains 
in every battle they fight.</i>

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:14.350
I've heard that communications 
in the vicinity of the fortress

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:17.030
have been disrupted for the past two days.

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:18.570
What is going on?

00:20:18.890 --> 00:20:21.310
It's clear the enemy is present,

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.860
but we are unable to locate 
them at the moment.

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:27.150
If the enemy is present, we must deploy.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.960
I just told you that we 
don't know where they are.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:32.290
Precisely why we must mobilize.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:35.600
To find where the enemy is hiding, as well.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:42.230
Your Excellency, we have a 
transmission on one of the lines.

00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:46.350
It is unstable and intermittent, however.

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:47.820
What is it saying?

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:48.630
Yes, sir.

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:55.070
"We are a force under secret orders to 
conduct espionage within Alliance territory.

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:59.520
During a mission, the rebels 
detected us, so we left the area,

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:04.050
but the Alliance fleet opened pursuit 
and engaged us in the corridor.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:08.090
Requesting immediate backup from 
Iserlohn." It ends there.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:14.320
We may not know where in the corridor 
they are, but we must deploy now!

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:17.040
Please wait, Your Excellency.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:21.270
Oberstein? Do you have something to say?

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:22.200
Yes, sir.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.000
Very well. Speak up.

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:26.600
Then, I will do so.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:28.960
I believe this is a trap.

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:30.050
A trap?

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:34.190
Yes, one intended to draw our 
fleet away from Iserlohn.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:36.320
We must not deploy.

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:38.900
We should wait and see what happens.

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.180
The enemy is waiting out there.

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:44.060
Are you saying we'll lose if we attack?

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:47.750
As soldiers, to fight is our duty.

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:50.580
Rather than seek a moment's tranquility,

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:53.320
we should be thinking about attacking 
and defeating our enemy.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:57.050
Besides, how can we abandon our 
own men when they're in trouble?

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:59.440
I don't know about that, Admiral Seeckt.

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:02.300
Your officer does have a point.

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:05.790
Since we don't know where they 
are, the risk is too great.

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.900
Why don't we wait a bit?

00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:12.300
No! The entire fleet will sortie as 
soon as preparations are complete!

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:25.350
I hope he comes back bloodied up a bit.

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:28.570
Another message from the same force.

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:32.470
"We will soon arrive in 
optical range of Iserlohn,

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:36.250
but the rebel force is still in 
pursuit. Requesting backup."

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:38.990
Very well. Prepare to attack.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:39.710
Sir.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.730
What is Seeckt doing?

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:44.030
Ships detected on screen!

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:45.520
Enlarge it.

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:49.600
Prepare to return fire!

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:06.070
Very smart. Looks like they 
know they're no match for us.

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.200
Another transmission from the same force.

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:09.770
Fine. Put it on the monitor.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:15.980
<i>This is Captain von Laeken.</i>

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:19.200
<i>I request an urgent audience 
with the commander.</i>

00:24:50.010 --> 00:25:00.010
<b>Episode 7</b>

00:24:50.010 --> 00:25:00.010
<b>The Capture of Iserlohn (Part 2)</b>

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:54.800
<i>Next time on </i>Legend of 
 the Galactic Heroes - Die Neue These<i>,</i>

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:59.710
<i>episode seven, "The Capture 
of Iserlohn (Part 2)."</i>
